From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 03:20:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ntfs: a directory listing reports an index error instead of
 ending

ntfs_readdir() turns every error it meets while walking the directory
index into the end of the directory: at its out label it marks the
listing finished and returns 0. An index block that cannot be read or
fails its checks (ntfs_index_next(), ntfs_index_walk_down(),
ntfs_ia_open(), the lookup that resumes a listing) therefore makes the
listing come back short with no error: on a volume whose first index
block of a 300-entry directory had an entries offset of 16, "ls"
printed 277 names and exited with 0, while a lookup or a create in the
same directory failed with EIO.

Return the error instead, and do not mark the listing finished, so
that the next call meets it again and the caller learns that the list
is incomplete. That next call must resume neither from the key saved
when an earlier call filled its buffer nor from the position of the
last entry returned: the first returns entries again, with positions
that grow on every call, so the listing never ends; the second returns
the last entry twice. So the key is dropped and the position moves past
the last entry walked: the next call walks the index from its start,
skips what was returned and meets the error before returning anything.
"ls" then prints the 277 names and "Input/output error", and exits
with 1.

The one error that keeps ending the listing as before is -ENOENT from
the lookup of the entry where the previous call stopped, which only
means that entry was removed in between.

Mainline has the same code (v7.4-rc2 and master of 2026-11-10); not
sent upstream yet.
---
 fs/ntfs/dir.c | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/fs/ntfs/dir.c b/fs/ntfs/dir.c
index 829cfa7106dd..3493c4b9f8f8 100644
--- a/fs/ntfs/dir.c
+++ b/fs/ntfs/dir.c
@@ -740,6 +740,7 @@ static int ntfs_readdir(struct file *file, struct dir_context *actor)
 	unsigned long index;
 	struct rb_root ra_root = RB_ROOT;
 	struct file_ra_state *ra;
+	bool resume_gone = false;
 
 	ntfs_debug("Entering for inode 0x%llx, fpos 0x%llx.",
 			ndir->mft_no, actor->pos);
@@ -817,6 +818,13 @@ static int ntfs_readdir(struct file *file, struct dir_context *actor)
 
 			goto nextdir;
 		} else {
+			/*
+			 * The entry the previous call stopped at is gone: the
+			 * listing ends there, as it always has. Any other
+			 * error is the index's and is reported below.
+			 */
+			if (err == -ENOENT)
+				resume_gone = true;
 			goto out;
 		}
 	} else if (!private) {
@@ -1009,10 +1017,31 @@ out:
 
 	if (err) {
 		if (private) {
+			if (!resume_gone) {
+				/*
+				 * The next call walks the index from its start
+				 * up to the position reached here and meets the
+				 * error again: a saved key may be that of an
+				 * entry returned before, and the last entry
+				 * returned must not be returned twice.
+				 */
+				kfree(private->key);
+				private->key = NULL;
+				if (ie_pos > actor->pos)
+					actor->pos = ie_pos;
+			}
 			private->curr_pos = actor->pos;
-			private->end_in_iterate = true;
+			/*
+			 * Only a resume entry that has gone ends the listing.
+			 * An index that cannot be read must not look like the
+			 * end of the directory: the error goes to the caller,
+			 * and again on the next call, so a listing never comes
+			 * back short without one.
+			 */
+			private->end_in_iterate = resume_gone;
 		}
-		err = 0;
+		if (resume_gone)
+			err = 0;
 	}
 	ntfs_index_ctx_put(ictx);
 	kfree(name);
